# Skill: set up a LiteLLM auto router

You are helping the user add an auto router to their LiteLLM proxy. An auto router is one
model name that classifies each request and forwards it to the cheapest model that can
handle it, so a trivial question does not pay for a frontier model.

Reference documentation: https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/proxy/auto_routing

This is an interactive setup. Ask the questions below, wait for answers, and never invent a
model the user does not already serve. Ask everything you still need in as few messages as
possible rather than one question at a time, and skip any question the user has already
answered in their request.

## 1. Find the models the proxy already serves

Locate the config the proxy loads: the file passed to `litellm --config`, commonly
`config.yaml` or `proxy_config.yaml`. Read its `model_list` and note every `model_name`
along with which are chat models and which are embedding models.

If a running proxy is reachable, `curl -s $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/v1/models -H "Authorization:
Bearer $LITELLM_API_KEY"` lists the same names. If the models are managed in the database
(added through the Admin UI or `/model/new`) and no `model_list` exists in a file, use that
listing and create the router through the management API in step 4.

Show the user the list you found. If you cannot find any models, stop and ask where the
config lives rather than guessing.

## 2. Ask for the router name

Ask what to call the router; it becomes the model name callers request. Suggest
`auto-router` as the default.

One constraint worth raising only if it applies: Claude Code and Claude Desktop only offer
model names that read as Anthropic models, so if the user plans to call the router from one
of those, the name has to contain `claude`, `anthropic`, or a family word such as `opus`,
`sonnet`, or `haiku`, and no other vendor's name. Point them at
https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/tutorials/claude_code_autorouter when that is the case.

## 3. Ask which model serves each tier

Propose an assignment from the models you found, then ask the user to confirm or change it:

- `SIMPLE`: the cheapest usable chat model
- `MEDIUM`: a mid-tier general model
- `COMPLEX`: the strongest general model
- `REASONING`: a reasoning model

Explain what each tier catches so the choice is informed: SIMPLE takes greetings and
one-line lookups, MEDIUM ordinary questions, COMPLEX multi-step or code-heavy work, and
REASONING anything asking to think step by step or prove something. The same model may
serve several tiers when the list has nothing better, and a tier can be a list of models
the router picks from at random. Also ask which tier should be the fallback when
classification fails; MEDIUM is the usual answer.

## 4. Write the config

Append one entry to `model_list`, changing nothing else in the file:

```yaml
  - model_name: <router name>
    litellm_params:
      model: auto_router/complexity_router
      complexity_router_config:
        tiers:
          SIMPLE:    <simple model>
          MEDIUM:    <medium model>
          COMPLEX:   <complex model>
          REASONING: <reasoning model>
      complexity_router_default_model: <fallback model>
```

For a database-managed proxy, create the same deployment through the management API
instead:

```bash
curl -X POST $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/model/new \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_MASTER_KEY" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"model_name": "<router name>", "litellm_params": {"model": "auto_router/complexity_router", "complexity_router_config": {"tiers": {"SIMPLE": "<simple model>", "MEDIUM": "<medium model>", "COMPLEX": "<complex model>", "REASONING": "<reasoning model>"}}, "complexity_router_default_model": "<fallback model>"}}'
```

## 5. State the defaults you are assuming

Everything not written above runs on its default. Tell the user these explicitly and offer
to change any of them now:

- Classification uses the built-in heuristic scorer: no extra API call, sub-millisecond,
  scores prompt length, code presence, reasoning markers, and technical terms. The
  alternative is an LLM classifier, which costs one small model call per request and reads
  the request more accurately.
- Session affinity is off, so every turn of a conversation is classified on its own merits
  and lands on the cheapest tier adequate for it. Turning it on (`session_affinity: true`,
  with `session_affinity_ttl_seconds` defaulting to 3600) pins a session to its first-turn
  model, which preserves provider prompt caches and avoids replaying one model's
  conversation history to another, at the cost of the whole session inheriting the first
  turn's tier.
- No keyword rules and no semantic matching, so nothing short-circuits the classifier.
  `keyword_tier_rules` can force chosen phrases into a tier, and
  `semantic_keyword_matching` extends that to paraphrases using an embedding model.
- Adaptive routing is off, so a tier holding several models picks among them uniformly at
  random rather than Thompson-sampling by observed quality.

If the user picks the LLM classifier, ask which model should classify (a small fast one,
chosen from the models they already serve) and add:

```yaml
        classifier_type: llm
        classifier_llm_config:
          model: <small fast model>
          timeout_ms: 2000
```

Then tell them what that turns on by default: the classifier sees the last 3 prior human
turns of the conversation, truncated to 200 characters each
(`classifier_context_window_size: 3`, `classifier_context_per_turn_chars: 200`), so a
follow-up like "now do the same for the streaming path" is rated against what it refers to.
Assistant replies are excluded (`classifier_context_include_assistant_turns: false`); the
setting exists for conversations where the assistant states the difficulty and the user
only answers "yes". Set the window size to 0 to classify the current message alone. Any
value left at its default should stay out of the config.

## 6. Verify

Restart the proxy, then send one trivial request and one hard one, and show the user both
responses and the model each landed on:

```bash
curl -s $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_API_KEY" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"model":"<router name>","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"what is 2+2?"}]}'

curl -s $LITELLM_PROXY_URL/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $LITELLM_API_KEY" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"model":"<router name>","messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Think step by step and design a multi-region active-active Postgres failover strategy."}]}'
```

The proxy logs carry one line per routing decision:

```
ComplexityRouter: routing decision cause=complexity_scorer, tier=SIMPLE, score=-0.150, signals=['short (7 tokens)', 'simple (what is)'], routed_model=gpt-4o-mini
```

The two requests should report different tiers. If they do not, show the user what you saw
rather than tuning thresholds on your own.

## 7. Hand back

Tell the user the router name to call, the tier assignments, the defaults in force, and
where the remaining knobs live:
https://docs.litellm.ai/docs/proxy/auto_routing#full-config
